WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.540
CMake is often treated as plumbing: useful, noisy, and separate from the application code that receives most review attention.

2
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.480
In a C++ project, that view is too narrow.

3
00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:24.460
The build system decides which source files become part of the product, which compiler and linker settings are active, which libraries are selected, which tests run, which artifacts are packaged, and which installation rules are applied.

4
00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:30.420
A small CMake change can alter the security posture of the delivered program.

5
00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:42.200
It might change a language standard, remove a warning setting, select a different dependency, turn off a defensive check for a release profile, or add a build-time script that produces generated source.

6
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.860
None of those changes need to look dramatic in a review to affect the final binary.

7
00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:52.420
Secure CMake work starts by treating configuration as part of the trusted engineering system.

8
00:00:52.520 --> 00:01:00.840
Build files should be readable, intentional, documented where policy matters, and reviewed with the same seriousness as production source.

9
00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:12.460
A reviewer should be able to answer basic questions: what is being built, which options apply, which dependency is linked, what leaves the packaging step, and which assumptions are platform-specific.

10
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.540
The goal is not to make CMake complicated.

11
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:20.040
The goal is to make build intent explicit and repeatable.

12
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:28.460
When teams can reproduce the same build profile in development and CI, inspect generated artifacts, and explain configuration choices, they reduce ambiguity.

13
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:38.800
That clarity supports safer releases, faster review, better incident response, and a stronger connection between source code quality and shipped application behavior.
